      "The Series 63 exam is mostly about state rules, not federal rules. You may still see a few questions on federal laws, such as the Securities Act of 1933 or the Investment Company Act of 1940.\n\nThe North American Securities Administrators Association (NASAA) writes model rules that states use. They include:\n• The Uniform Securities Act (USA)\n• The Uniform Prudent Investor Act\n• Other NASAA model rules\n\nThese rules protect investors and stop fraud in three main ways:\n• Registering investment professionals and their firms.\n• Registering certain securities sold to people in the state.\n• Setting ethical standards so the market is fair for everyone.\n\nYou may hear words like financial planner, wealth manager, or stockbroker. They will not be on the exam. Instead, the exam uses two terms. An investment adviser representative (IAR) works for an investment adviser or a federal covered adviser. An agent works for a broker-dealer.\n\nThe exam also talks about the \"Administrator\" or \"State Securities Administrator.\" This is the office in a state's government that oversees securities. The Administrator is a \"person\" under the law, but it is not an individual.",
